<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01DG">Definition <span data-tag="01DG">4.4.4.1</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, let $X$ be a simplicial set, and suppose we are given a pair of diagrams $f,f': X \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. A <em>natural transformation from $f$ to $f'$</em> is a morphism $u: f \rightarrow f'$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Fun}(X, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$. A <em>natural isomorphism from $f$ to $f'$</em> is a natural transformation $u: f \rightarrow f'$ which is an isomorphism in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{Fun}(X, \operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$ (Definition <a data-tag="004R" href="/tag/004R">1.4.6.1</a>). We say that $f$ and $f'$ are <em>naturally isomorphic</em> if there exists a natural isomorphism from $f$ to $f'$. </p>
